\section{Hide Representation, Not Responsibility}

A C API can forward declare an incomplete type and return a
pointer to it. The caller can hold that pointer and pass it
to declared operations, but need not know the object's size
or fields. This is a native opaque handle, distinct from a
runtime handle referring to a managed object. The C API survey
uses opaque JNI reference types for the second direction; the
Lua embedding extract illustrates userdata as a place a host
can associate native data with a VM value. Neither technique
answers who owes destruction of a particular native object.
An opaque pointer is not automatically an owned pointer.

Consider a native music catalog with an owned session and
read-only track views borrowed from that session. A session
can close explicitly. A view can be used only while its
session exists, and becomes invalid on a documented refresh.
The wrapper should not expose the view solely as a pointer
with a destructor; it acquires no right to free an individual
track. A public view may carry a strong reference to the
session plus a refresh generation. A query borrows the
session for the duration of native use and validates the
generation under the same synchronization that excludes
refresh. A strong reference alone does not prevent explicit
close or structural mutation.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lp{0.60\linewidth}}
\hline
Returned handle & Wrapper obligation \\
\hline
Fresh owner & Invoke matching destroy exactly once. \\
Retained shared handle & Balance each successful retain with release. \\
Borrowed view & Hold a parent lease; check the view generation. \\
Static borrowed handle & Do not free; honor module and library lifetime. \\
Ownership transferred out & Mark wrapper moved only when transfer commits. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Use a State Machine Rather Than a Null Check}

The address of an allocation may be recycled. Its numeric
identity cannot distinguish a new session from an old one.
An internal handle table can pair slot indexes with generations,
refuse stale tokens, and avoid claiming that tokens are stable
across process invocations. Do not silently recycle a
generation on wraparound while an old token may survive.
Native addresses, if used at all, remain private to the raw
wrapper and must be presented only to operations of the right
handle family and library instance.

\Cref{fig:visual-394} illustrates an owner with nonblocking
close. Close rejects future uses but need not destroy the
resource immediately. Active leases can finish, then the
last one schedules destruction on the owner thread. Explicit
close and finalization share the same state transition, so
they cannot each destroy the pointer. A finalizer should not
be relied on for timely release; files, device slots and
registrations can be exhausted long before a collector
notices memory pressure \cite{jones2011garbage}.

