\section{Make Lease Acquisition Atomic With Admission}

\begin{verbatim}
acquire(owner):
    with owner.lock:
        if owner.state != Open: return Closed
        owner.active += 1
        return Lease(owner, owner.ptr)

close(owner):
    with owner.lock:
        if owner.state == Open: owner.state := Closing
        task := take_destroy_task_if_ready_locked(owner)
    if task: queue_to_owner_thread(task)

release(lease):
    owner := lease.owner
    with owner.lock:
        owner.active -= 1
        task := take_destroy_task_if_ready_locked(owner)
    if task: queue_to_owner_thread(task)

take_destroy_task_if_ready_locked(owner):
    if owner.state != Closing or owner.active != 0: return None
    owner.state := DestroyQueued
    return retained_destroy_task(owner, move(owner.ptr))
\end{verbatim}

Assume leases are single-use and that installing a lease is
infallible once the count is incremented. Reserve any required
storage before acquiring the lock. The queue accepts the task
without throwing or uses a specified safe fallback.
The job calls the native destroy once, outside the lock and
on the correct thread, then records \texttt{Destroyed}.
Use a destroy API whose lifetime effect is defined even if
it also reports a diagnostic; a failure with ownership
retained needs a separate recovery state. Merely clearing
the pointer could lose the only remaining owner. Never hold
the state mutex across a C operation that can invoke host
callbacks, including a destroy operation with that behavior.

Crestani's local and global reference lifetimes concern
managed referents visible to C, but the distinction helps
here too: a temporary reference that proves reachability
for one call is not a permanent ownership claim. If the
catalog retains a managed callback, hold that callback
root independently of the individual catalog operations
until native quiescence, then release it. Closing a catalog
does not imply callback release unless the native contract
guarantees the appropriate join.

\section{Find the Transfer Point on Each Status Branch}

Suppose registering a native filter in the catalog consumes
it only on success. Keep a private owner before the call,
prevent simultaneous loans or transfers, and move its
pointer to the catalog only on the success branch. On
failure it remains an owned filter, still eligible for a
retry or for destruction. State the opposite explicitly
for a native API that consumes its argument on all returns.
Without the status-specific rule the wrapper either frees
a pointer it no longer owns or leaks a filter still owed.

Test an allocator that reuses a released address, a view
whose generation invalidates on refresh without moving
the underlying allocation, close racing a blocked lease,
and a destroy operation observed on the owner thread.
For transfer, run separate success and failure fixtures
that count frees at the supplier and at the wrapper.
Check that the sum is one after all objects are closed.

\section{Avoid a Strong Reference Cycle Through a Callback}

Suppose each catalog holds a native subscription whose
context roots a managed closure which captures the owning
catalog. The collector considers the root strong, so
dropping the last application reference to the catalog will
not run its finalizer. Destroying the subscription only
from that finalizer cannot break the cycle. Introduce an
explicit subscription lifetime, or root a dispatcher with
a weak reference to the catalog when loss of the owner
means delivery should stop. A weak lookup must be upgraded
to a temporary strong lease before invoking application
code. If it fails, the invocation returns the native stop
result and initiates teardown via the designated owner.

Design the library unloading rule together with this
teardown path. A queued destruction job keeps the module
lease independently of whether there is still a reachable
catalog object. When running the job, first ensure native
subscriptions cannot invoke any longer, then call destroy
and release the lease. If the runtime stops processing
that queue before draining it, assigning destruction to
the correct thread on paper accomplishes nothing. Verify
shutdown while an application intentionally forgets to close
one subscription and a destruction task is already queued.
The runtime may provide explicit leak reporting rather
than pretend that forgotten close is timely or harmless.

A useful debug assertion is that \texttt{DestroyQueued} owns
the only destroy task and has zero active leases. Assert it
when dequeuing, not merely when closing, so tests can catch
a misguided path that reopened admission after scheduling.
